        "text_block_ecNyzQ": {
          "type": "text_block",
          "settings": {
            "enable_image": true,
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/porto-fabrics.jpg",
            "image_width": 700,
            "title": "Porto",
            "text": "<p>Porto is undergoing a tourism-driven transformation. With 122 new hotel projects currently underway, the city will soon add nearly 12,000 new beds to its existing 23,000-bed capacity—21,500 of which are in hotels [3].<br/><br/></p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        },
        "text_block_EYyJqr": {
          "type": "text_block",
          "settings": {
            "enable_image": true,
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/villa-foz-hotel-fabrics-spa2_81e4ae44-4163-47f0-90cb-c0d8bb535c23.jpg",
            "image_width": 700,
            "title": "Hotel",
            "text": "<p>These new developments alone will require an estimated 71 to 111 tons of upholstery fabric. When regular refurbishments of the existing hotel stock are considered, the demand—and environmental impact—grows substantially.<br/><br/></p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        },
        "text_block_jfGqht": {
          "type": "text_block",
          "settings": {
            "enable_image": true,
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/sustainable-fabrics-portugal_fe5cd184-16fa-4316-be37-109561fcb464.jpg",
            "image_width": 700,
            "title": "Sustainablility",
            "text": "<p>By choosing a bio-based alternative like PEEL over conventional synthetics, Porto’s hospitality sector could avoid using 48 to 74 tons of fossil-derived raw materials. This would be a meaningful step toward a more sustainable, climate-conscious urban future.</p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        }
